in @prism
in @glass
in @meta
in @spectral
in @spectral/gen_prism
in @spectral/supervisor
in @uuid/spectral
in @mirror/store

# @spectral/registry — the supervisor's typed child index.
#
# THE THIRD SUB-SHARD of the @spectral runtime cascade. Sibling of
# `shards/spectral/gen_prism.mirror` (commit ae965ca, the worker
# primitive) and `shards/spectral/supervisor.mirror` (commit 452ccb2,
# the lifecycle-owner specialisation whose `base.state: shard_ref`
# field is the substrate-altitude HANDLE to the registry shard).
# This tick lifts the registry's typed QUERY SURFACE to the substrate
# altitude.
#
# Per docs/specs/spectral-runtime.md §3 (2026-06-10, Mara): the
# supervisor's state surface IS the registry shard — a content-
# addressed index from child `uuid_spectral` to child `shard_ref`.
# The supervisor's invariant fires on each child against this index;
# restart-driven transitions ARE lattice ascent against the
# registry's settled state (per
# [[architecture-shard-as-crdt]]).
#
# === The forward-promise this tick closes ===
#
# `shards/spectral/supervisor.mirror` (commit 452ccb2) declared:
#
#   "The dedicated registry shard (`shards/spectral/registry.mirror`)
#    carries the registry's typed surface (lookup-by-uuid, enumerate
#    children, observe child state). This shard does NOT inline the
#    registry contract; it forward-promises it. The supervisor's
#    `base.state: shard_ref` field is the substrate-altitude handle
#    to the registry shard at runtime; the dedicated shard fills in
#    the query surface in a later cascade tick."
#
# That tick is this tick. The supervisor's lifecycle surface
# (start_child / terminate_child) does NOT depend on the registry's
# query surface; the substrate-pull cascade pulled lifecycle first,
# query surface second. The supervisor's `base.state: shard_ref`
# stays as it is — this shard is the typed surface OVER that handle.
#
# === The keyword decision ===
#
# `glass` per recognition #46 (path-syntax property; promoted) and
# `shards/spectral.mirror`'s discipline section: "@spectral
# sub-shards at depth >= 1 use `glass`; the family root itself uses
# `prism` per the depth-0 discipline."
#
# Why NOT a new substrate keyword: registry is a sub-prism
# specialisation of the supervisor's state surface — the BEAM
# `Registry` analogue — and `glass` already names that relationship
# per `shards/glass.mirror`'s `glass(name) -> prism` form. Same
# reasoning as gen_prism (ae965ca) and supervisor (452ccb2): not a
# new structural kind, but a typed surface OVER an existing carrier
# (the supervisor's `base.state: shard_ref`).
#
# === The registry shape — base + entries ===
#
# The supervisor's `base.state` IS the registry shard at runtime;
# the dedicated registry declaration lifts that runtime fact to a
# typed substrate record. The record carries the `shard_ref` handle
# (the substrate-altitude pointer to the supervisor's state shard
# at @mirror/store) and the entry list (the typed index that
# resolves through the handle).
#
#   base    — `shard_ref`. The supervisor's state surface. The same
#              carrier the supervisor's `base.state` field carries
#              (per shards/spectral/supervisor.mirror's `type
#              supervisor = { base: gen_prism, ... }` with
#              `base.state: shard_ref` resolving through gen_prism's
#              embedded state surface). Substrate-pull rationale: NO
#              second handle. The registry IS the state surface; the
#              substrate's load-bearing identity for the registry IS
#              the supervisor's state shard_ref. Two carriers for the
#              same thing would proliferate identity.
#
#   entries — `[registry_entry]`. The typed index. One entry per
#              currently-registered child. Mutated under the
#              supervisor's restart-strategy invariant; observable
#              through the registry's five-op block.
#
# === The entry shape ===
#
#   id     — the child's `uuid_spectral`. Both halves load-bearing
#              per shards/spectral/gen_prism.mirror's identity
#              surface: the ACTIVE 48 bits place the gen_prism in
#              the substrate's spectral manifold (Fate's mycelial
#              routing reads through); the DARK 80 bits carry
#              identity (the lookup key). `lookup` discriminates on
#              byte-equality of the dark portion.
#
#   child  — the child `gen_prism` handle. NOT a `uuid_spectral`
#              alone: the registry's load-bearing entry is the full
#              child record (identity + state + parent), because the
#              registry's observable surface IS lookup-and-observe.
#              A consumer that calls `lookup(r, id)` and recovers a
#              `gen_prism` can then call the gen_prism's five-op
#              surface against the recovered handle — that's the
#              query-then-observe pattern the registry exists to
#              serve. A consumer that wants ONLY the uuid_spectral
#              recovers `child.identity` from the returned gen_prism.
#
# === The :via field — surfaced but NOT landed ===
#
# BEAM's registered-name pattern (`:via` tuple lookup against a
# `Registry` process; modern Elixir's `{:via, Registry, ...}`) is
# an optimisation layer OVER the uuid_spectral-keyed registry — a
# secondary lookup mechanism for callers that hold a human-named
# handle rather than a substrate-content-addressed identity. The
# brief sketch listed a `via: ref` field on `registry_entry`.
#
# Substrate-pull-correct decision (STOP-condition 3 surfaced and
# resolved here): the substrate does NOT have a `registered_name`
# type distinct from `uuid_spectral`. The substrate's identity
# discipline is content-addressed all the way down (per
# [[feedback-no-bare-types]], [[feedback-no-stringly-types]]); a
# secondary `ref`-typed name field on every entry would either be
# (a) bare `ref` (stringly), or (b) a typed `registered_name`
# carrier that does not yet exist. Both routes are wrong: (a)
# violates substrate type discipline; (b) introduces a carrier
# without a substrate-altitude declaration of what it IS.
#
# This tick DROPS the `via` field. If the substrate later needs
# named-handle lookup (e.g., for a CLI's `mirror sup show NAME`
# command, or a debugger that wants to attach by name), that lift
# happens at a separate sub-prism — `@spectral/registry/via` or
# `@spectral/name` — where the typed registered-name carrier gains
# its own declaration alongside a `lookup_by_name` surface. The
# present registry contract is the CORE substrate surface (per
# docs/specs/spectral-runtime.md §4: insert / remove / lookup /
# list against uuid_spectral); BEAM's `:via` is the optimisation,
# not the contract.
#
# Per docs/specs/spectral-runtime.md §3 (the substrate's restart
# invariant): the supervisor's restart logic ONLY needs the
# uuid_spectral-keyed index — the dark 80 bits identify the
# process for parentage, restart, and lookup. The named-handle
# concern is downstream of substrate.
#
# === The query surface — insert / remove / lookup / list ===
#
# Per docs/specs/spectral-runtime.md §4 (registry has insert /
# remove / query / list contracts) lifted to the substrate-pull-
# correct vocabulary:
#
#   insert(r, e) -> registry
#     Append a `registry_entry` to the registry. Returns a NEW
#     registry — registry is a value record; the supervisor's state
#     surface advances by one settle (the registry's settled state
#     advances by one lattice element per
#     [[architecture-shard-as-crdt]]). Body discharges by composing
#     the entry into the registry's settled state shard at
#     @mirror/store.
#
#     Why NOT mutate in place: the substrate has no in-place
#     mutation discipline; the registry IS a content-addressed
#     shard at every observable transition (per gen_prism's
#     substrate-pull contract: every observable transition is
#     store-visible).
#
#   remove(r, id) -> registry
#     Drop the entry with `entry.id == id`. Returns a NEW registry.
#     If no entry matches, returns `r` unchanged (the substrate's
#     idempotent-on-miss discipline; remove-of-absent is a no-op).
#     Body discharges by composing the registry's settled state
#     shard at @mirror/store minus the dropped entry.
#
#     Substrate-pull rationale for the unchanged-on-miss return
#     (not `imperfect`): the registry's CORRECTNESS does not depend
#     on the entry having existed pre-call — the postcondition is
#     "entry with id is not in r". A miss satisfies the
#     postcondition without ceremony. Compare with `lookup` below,
#     where the absence IS the substrate-altitude question.
#
#   lookup(r, id) -> imperfect
#     Discriminate on byte-equality of the dark 80 bits per
#     uuid_spectral's identity_signal (shards/uuid/spectral.mirror).
#     The substrate's three-state outcome carrier per @glass:
#
#       success(gen_prism) — entry exists; gen_prism handle returned.
#       partial(...)        — entry partially observable (e.g., the
#                              registry shard is mid-settle; the
#                              entry's gen_prism record references
#                              a not-yet-fetched state shard at
#                              @mirror/store). opacity_map names
#                              the unsettled portion; kintsugi
#                              re-reads to drive the partial to
#                              success.
#       failure(reason)     — entry not in registry. The substrate's
#                              standard "not found" with reason
#                              carrier per @glass.
#
#     Why `imperfect` and not a bare `gen_prism`: the registry's
#     lookup is the QUESTION the supervisor's invariant asks of its
#     state. A bare `gen_prism` return forces the caller to invent
#     an absent-handle (null-equivalent), which violates the
#     substrate's three-state discipline. `imperfect` carries the
#     question's verdict at the type level.
#
#   list(r) -> [registry_entry]
#     Enumerate the registry's entries. Returns the entire entry
#     list (the supervisor's view of "who am I supervising right
#     now"). Body discharges by reading the registry's settled
#     state shard at @mirror/store and projecting the entry list.
#
#     Why NOT a streaming/paginated surface: the substrate's
#     query discipline is set-at-once. The supervisor's child set
#     is bounded by the parent gen_prism's child_specs list (per
#     shards/spectral/supervisor.mirror); a supervisor with
#     unboundedly-many children is itself a substrate anomaly the
#     Hamilton-scheduler discipline catches at a higher altitude
#     (per [[architecture-hamilton-scheduler]] — Apollo-1202
#     priority discharge). The list surface returns the entire
#     set; downstream consumers paginate at their boundary.
#
# The list ordering is the registry shard's settled order (per
# [[architecture-shard-as-crdt]]: children sorted by content; the
# CRDT discipline guarantees deterministic ordering across
# observation points).
#
# === Sheaf reading: NOT here, it's at entanglement ===
#
# Per docs/specs/spectral-runtime.md §4 and
# [[Eigenboard is a sheaf]]: the cellular-sheaf restriction map
# structure lives at the ENTANGLEMENT edge class, not the
# registry. Entanglement is the peer-correlation edge (orthogonal
# to parentage; polyadic; cyclic permitted); the registry is the
# typed CHILD INDEX (parentage-side; one parent per child; acyclic).
#
# Architectural note (NOT a STOP, but a surface): the registry
# COULD be read as a degenerate sheaf — each entry IS a
# restriction map from the supervisor's state to the child's state
# along the parentage edge. That reading is consistent with
# [[Eigenboard is a sheaf]]'s cellular-sheaf framing but NOT
# load-bearing at this tick. The substrate's sheaf surface lives
# at `shards/spectral/entanglement.mirror` (forward-promised). The
# registry's typed surface stays at the index discipline.
#
# === Honest partial discipline (per #306) ===
#
# The action bodies below all carry `\` obligation blocks — no
# fracture body emits any morphism in this tick. No `partials`
# declaration needed (per `dissonance_partials_match_ast_breadth`
# #306). Bodies discharge downstream when the consumer pulls
# realisation through `@code/metalogue/materialize` (per the
# ouroboros pipeline in docs/specs/spectral-runtime.md §5).
#
# === Cascade siblings (forward-promised, NOT landing this tick) ===
#
#   shards/spectral/parent.mirror         single-parent lifecycle
#                                          edge type (acyclic).
#                                          STOP-condition 2/4: the
#                                          acyclic invariant wants
#                                          its own property/fracture
#                                          bilateral pair
#                                          (analogous to
#                                          `keyword_matches_depth`
#                                          per
#                                          [[architecture-property-fracture-bilateral]]).
#                                          That's a separate
#                                          bilateral instance, not a
#                                          simple type declaration;
#                                          it lands as its own tick;
#   shards/spectral/entanglement.mirror   peer correlation edge;
#                                          sheaf restriction map at
#                                          substrate altitude
#                                          (cellular sheaf per
#                                          [[Eigenboard is a sheaf]]);
#   shards/spectral/root.mirror           parentless supervisor;
#                                          vocabulary anchor at
#                                          ~/.mirror/;
#   shards/spectral/portal.mirror         runtime side of portal
#                                          (gen_prism specialisation
#                                          carrying the frame stream).
#
# Spec context: docs/specs/spectral-runtime.md (the ouroboros
#               spec; §3 supervision tree — supervisor's state IS
#               registry; §4 entanglement graph — sheaf reading
#               lives at entanglement, NOT registry; §8 cascade
#               map).
# Substrate decisions: [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
#                      [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]],
#                      [[architecture-three-tier-stack]] (the
#                      SpectralSupervisor precedent; the registry
#                      is the supervisor's typed query surface),
#                      [[architecture-shard-as-crdt]] (the registry
#                      state surface IS a shard; insert / remove
#                      ARE lattice ascent),
#                      [[architecture-operations-as-linear-algebra]]
#                      (the runtime reading of the five ops the
#                      glass declaration carries),
#                      [[Eigenboard is a sheaf]] (the architectural
#                      surface: registry is the parentage-edge
#                      index; sheaf restriction maps live at
#                      entanglement; degenerate sheaf reading of
#                      the registry surfaced but not load-bearing),
#                      [[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-word]]
#                      (the registry name lived in the
#                      SpectralSupervisor memory and the
#                      docs/specs/spectral-runtime.md §3/§4 spec
#                      since the supervisor's lift; this tick names
#                      the parent at the substrate altitude — the
#                      54th-instance rug-pull),
#                      [[feedback-no-bare-types]] (the `via` field's
#                      typed-name discipline rationale; bare `ref`
#                      stringly violates substrate type-floor).
# Related shards: spectral.mirror (the family root this glass is
#                  under),
#                 spectral/gen_prism.mirror (the worker primitive
#                  the registry indexes; commit ae965ca; the entry
#                  child field carries this type),
#                 spectral/supervisor.mirror (the lifecycle-owner
#                  whose base.state IS the registry shard; commit
#                  452ccb2; this shard fills in supervisor's
#                  forward-promise),
#                 uuid/spectral.mirror (the identity carrier; the
#                  registry's lookup key IS uuid_spectral; the
#                  dark 80 bits discriminate),
#                 mirror/store.mirror (the registry's state surface
#                  carrier; shard_ref = uuid_spectral; the
#                  supervisor's base.state resolves through
#                  @mirror/store.read),
#                 glass.mirror (the imperfect carrier for lookup's
#                  three-state outcome).
# Path-namespace property: this file at
# `shards/spectral/registry.mirror` declares
# `@spectral/registry` and only that.

glass @spectral/registry {
  focus registry
  project registry
  split registry
  shift registry
  settle registry
}

# === registry_entry — one supervised child ===
#
# Per docs/specs/spectral-runtime.md §3 — the supervisor's invariant
# fires on each child against its entry. One entry per currently-
# registered child.
#
#   id      — the child's uuid_spectral. The lookup key. Both halves
#              load-bearing per shards/spectral/gen_prism.mirror's
#              identity discipline. Discrimination is on byte-equality
#              of the dark 80 bits (the identity_signal per
#              shards/uuid/spectral.mirror).
#   child   — the full gen_prism handle. The registry's load-bearing
#              observable surface IS the recovered gen_prism record
#              (identity + state + parent surfaces) — callers project
#              child.state to read state, child.parent to read the
#              lifecycle edge, child.identity to recover the
#              uuid_spectral they queried with.
#
# NOT carried: a `via` field (the BEAM registered-name pattern). The
# substrate has no typed `registered_name` carrier distinct from
# uuid_spectral; adding one as bare `ref` would violate the substrate's
# type-floor discipline. The named-handle concern lifts as a separate
# sub-prism (`@spectral/registry/via` or `@spectral/name`) when its
# first consumer surfaces.
type registry_entry = {
  id:    uuid_spectral,
  child: gen_prism,
}

# === registry — the supervisor's typed child index ===
#
# Per docs/specs/spectral-runtime.md §3: the supervisor's state
# surface IS this registry. The `base` field carries the supervisor's
# state-shard handle (the same `shard_ref` the supervisor's
# `base.state` field already names per
# shards/spectral/supervisor.mirror); the `entries` field carries the
# typed index that resolves through the handle.
#
#   base    — shard_ref. The supervisor's state surface. SAME
#              carrier as supervisor.base.state; NO second identity.
#   entries — [registry_entry]. The typed index. Mutated under the
#              supervisor's restart-strategy invariant; insert /
#              remove ARE lattice ascent against the registry shard
#              at @mirror/store.
#
# The registry IS the substrate-altitude form of "who is this
# supervisor supervising right now" — the typed view OVER the
# settled state shard the supervisor carries as its observable
# state.
type registry = {
  base:    shard_ref,
  entries: [registry_entry],
}

# === insert — append an entry; advance the registry's state ===
#
# Append a `registry_entry` to the registry. Returns a NEW registry
# whose settled state shard at @mirror/store advances by one lattice
# element (per [[architecture-shard-as-crdt]]). The supervisor's
# `base.state` advances accordingly; the supervisor's observable
# state-shard transitions from the prior registry to the new one.
#
# Per the substrate's no-mutation discipline: every observable
# transition produces a settled shard; the registry is content-
# addressed at every step (the `base: shard_ref` after insert is a
# different uuid_spectral from the pre-insert `base`).
#
# Body discharges by composing the entry into the registry's
# settled state shard at @mirror/store and re-settling. The body
# stays a `\` obligation in this tick; concrete resolution lives at
# the realisation layer (via @code/metalogue/materialize per the
# ouroboros pipeline).
insert(r: registry, e: registry_entry) -> registry { \ }

# === remove — drop the entry with a matching id ===
#
# Drop the entry whose `id` byte-equals the argument. Returns a NEW
# registry. If no entry matches, returns `r` unchanged (the
# substrate's idempotent-on-miss discipline; remove-of-absent is a
# no-op satisfying the postcondition "entry with id is not in r").
#
# Body discharges by composing the registry's settled state shard
# at @mirror/store minus the dropped entry and re-settling. The
# body stays a `\` obligation in this tick.
remove(r: registry, id: uuid_spectral) -> registry { \ }

# === lookup — discriminate on the registry's three-state outcome ===
#
# Discriminate on byte-equality of the dark 80 bits per
# uuid_spectral's identity_signal. The substrate's three-state
# outcome carrier per @glass:
#
#   success(gen_prism) — entry exists; gen_prism handle returned.
#   partial(...)        — entry partially observable (registry shard
#                          mid-settle; opacity_map names the
#                          unsettled portion; kintsugi re-reads).
#   failure(reason)     — entry not in registry. Standard "not
#                          found" with reason carrier per @glass.
#
# The substrate's QUESTION discipline: lookup is the supervisor's
# invariant asking "does this child exist in my registry?" and the
# answer is verdict-laden, not a bare reference. `imperfect`
# carries the verdict at the type level (per
# [[architecture-error-as-question]]: errors are questions
# Reflection answers).
#
# Body discharges by reading the registry's settled state shard at
# @mirror/store, discriminating on the dark 80 bits, lifting the
# matching entry's `child` into success / partial / failure. Stays
# a `\` obligation in this tick.
lookup(r: registry, id: uuid_spectral) -> imperfect { \ }

# === list — enumerate the registry's entries ===
#
# Returns the entire entry list — the supervisor's view of "who am I
# supervising right now." The list ordering is the registry shard's
# settled order (per [[architecture-shard-as-crdt]]: children sorted
# by content; the CRDT discipline guarantees deterministic ordering
# across observation points).
#
# Set-at-once, not paginated: the substrate's query discipline is
# bounded set; the supervisor's child set is bounded by the parent
# gen_prism's child_specs list. Downstream consumers paginate at
# their boundary.
#
# Body discharges by reading the registry's settled state shard at
# @mirror/store and projecting the entry list. Stays a `\`
# obligation in this tick.
list(r: registry) -> [registry_entry] { \ }

out @spectral/registry
out registry_entry
out registry
out insert
out remove
out lookup
out list
